  "transcript": "# National Resilience\n\n## How aware is the public?\n> Public awareness of national and interconnected risks is generally low and focused on immediate local threats.\n\nChair: To start: how aware is the public of connected national and international risks, including those tied to communications?\nNihal Newman: Ofcom mostly talks to the industry we regulate, not the public directly, but we do publish consumer guidance and sector risk reports like Connected Nations to raise awareness.\nChair: You said you monitor industry compliance—how compliant is industry?\nNihal Newman: Telecoms are following a journey under the Telecommunications (Security) Act. Operators are taking steps to meet requirements, but threats evolve so it’s ongoing.\nProfessor Lee Miles: From the public’s side, awareness is low. In our 2025 UK household study, only about half recognised hazards and many took no action—21% saw no risks at all.\nLord Oates: Are those household perceptions accurate—do they match real risks?\nProfessor Lee Miles: Perception, preparedness and outcomes differ. People focus on local, personal risks—home accidents topped the list—so national threats can seem distant and get little action.\n\n\n## Getting the message across\n> Resilience communications must translate national risks into everyday choices, use many platforms, and be well resourced.\n\nBaroness Northover: With a fragmented media landscape and young people using social platforms, how do we communicate resilience effectively?\nProfessor Lee Miles: Treat people as rational: they prioritise local risks. I suggest the 'four Es'—enlighten (explain why resilience matters), empower choices, enable action (incentives/support), and evaluate outcomes.\nProfessor Lee Miles: Use a hub-and-spoke model: a central agreed message (hub) tailored and delivered on many platforms (spokes) from TikTok to local groups. That’s not cheap—it needs resources.\nNihal Newman: Ofcom does consumer research, such as Media Nations; I can share that to help understand where people actually get information.\nBaroness Hunter of Auchenreoch: What international examples could the UK use—Nordic countries, Africa, Caribbean?\nProfessor Lee Miles: Nordic countries benefit from high trust and a total defence tradition, so simple tools like crisis leaflets work there. But the UK has different social trust levels, so we must adapt, not copy.\nProfessor Lee Miles: In parts of Africa and the Caribbean, low‑tech redundancy—community hailer systems, local plans—matter because high‑tech backup isn’t available; the UK should keep low‑tech redundancy for rural or cut‑off areas.\n\n\n## If the networks fail: blackouts, satellites and cyber\n> Planning must assume prolonged outages; telecoms and energy must coordinate, and laws and exercises are improving resilience but must stay agile.\n\nLord Farmer: If there was a multi-day communications blackout, and DTT is switched off in future, how would we warn the public or restore services?\nNihal Newman: Operators are expected to avoid single points of failure: build redundancy, test business continuity plans, and have power backups. In an outage they can deploy mobile cell sites and generators; government has satellite options and the Emergency Services Network for critical users.\nNihal Newman: Restoration depends on co‑operation with the energy sector to get power to sites quickly. Some communities already use satellite terminals for local resilience.\nLord Farmer: If satellites themselves were attacked or disabled, how resilient are we?\nNihal Newman: It’s layered: fixed, mobile, satellite—when gaps exceed commercial capacity, national arrangements led by the Cabinet Office and DSIT come into play.\nBaroness Mobarik: How much of emergency communications depends on commercial networks and satellites, and what sovereign options exist?\nNihal Newman: I can provide exact figures later. Sovereignty is debated—trusted partners versus fully domestic systems. The UK is building layers of resilience and partnerships to reduce single‑country reliance.\nBaroness Curran: Has Ofcom taken part in war‑scale contingency planning and exercises?\nNihal Newman: Yes—we work with DSIT, Cabinet Office and industry on cross‑sector scenarios and exercises and attend those national conversations.\nProfessor Lee Miles: Two points: how long an outage lasts matters—Caribbean examples show weeks without comms and months without power. Second, public expectations: many treat internet as essential, so expectation management is vital.\nProfessor Lee Miles: Also remember most recovery work is done by private companies; the arrangements and costs for long recovery matter beyond short MoUs.\nLord Peach: On cybersecurity: is the Government right to be proud? Are national exercises frequent and tough enough?\nNihal Newman: The Telecommunications (Security) Act (2021) came from NCSC threat work and gives a proportionate framework. Ofcom monitors compliance, and the government is updating the code of practice. For infrastructure beyond telecoms, the Cyber Security and Resilience Bill aims to set baselines and include data centres.\nBaroness Winterton of Doncaster: Why bring data centres into legislation and what will the new Bill add?\nNihal Newman: Data centres are designated critical infrastructure; they underpin AI and many services. The Bill aims for oversight, better incident reporting, and attention to supply‑chain risks; details will be in secondary legislation.\nBaroness Mobarik: SMEs face rising attacks—does Ofcom focus on them?\nNihal Newman: The code targets larger operators (turnover thresholds) but we engage smaller providers, share guidance and hold roundtables. NCSC publishes practical advice for smaller firms.\nProfessor Lee Miles: Codes must be agile: AI is changing attacks rapidly. Many SMEs report cyber incidents; training, simulations and educating senior management on strategic investment are urgent.\nBaroness Winterton of Doncaster: Is there risk of delay while secondary legislation and training wait to be finalised?\nNihal Newman: We already apply the Cyber Assessment Framework in practice. Putting it in law strengthens it, but the standards are already being used—work isn’t waiting for the Bill to start.\n\n\n## Education, TV switch‑off and final recommendations\n> Schools and FE/HE must teach resilience; removing terrestrial TV needs clear resilient replacements; prime recommendations are whole‑society coordination and rebuilding public trust.\n\nLord Oates: What role should education play at all levels in resilience?\nProfessor Lee Miles: Education is essential: schools can teach everyday resilience and citizenship, FE can provide practical skills and access courses, and universities provide specialist training, research and the planners of the future.\nChair: Is switching off digital terrestrial TV sensible given some groups rely on it?\nProfessor Lee Miles: Switching it off risks losing a universal resilience channel. If we remove it, we must clearly and convincingly replace it with services that work for older, rural or digitally excluded people and prove the replacements are resilient 24/7.\nNihal Newman: Technology evolves, so change may be sensible, but governments must guarantee resilience where needed and target extra protections for communities that would be disadvantaged.\nBaroness Helic: If you could give one recommendation to government, what would it be?\nNihal Newman: Make the 'whole‑society' approach real: bring government, industry, communities, and academia together around clear plans and shared responsibilities.\nProfessor Lee Miles: Focus on rebuilding public trust. A whole‑society approach only works if people trust institutions and messages—invest in everyday resilience that households understand and trust.\nChair: Thank you both—this highlights communications, cross‑sector working and trust as central to national resilience.\n",
